.PP
The Windows \f[CR]USER32.DLL\f[R] API is exposed through a single global
structure named \f[CR]USER32\f[R].
.PP
This structure is populated at runtime using
\f[CR]KERNEL32.LoadLibraryA\f[R], so some function pointers may be
\f[CR]NULL\f[R] depending on the target Windows version or execution
environment.
So, always verify that a function pointer is valid before calling it.
.PP
Available functions are declared in \f[CR]USER32.H\f[R].
You can extend the struct manually if you need additional exports that
are not currently included.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<CMT/WIN_CORE/USER32.H>\f[R]

int main()
{
    USER32.MessageBoxA(0, \(dqHello, World!\(dq, \(dqCMT\(dq, 0);
    \f[B]return\f[R] (0);
}
